        <!-- this entry is needed for IDEA v12. The version element the pre-migration file carried is kept, but
            it now references the reactor-wide groovy.version property instead of the literal 1.8.6: a version
            declared on a direct dependency wins over dependencyManagement, so a literal here would keep
            resolving groovy-all 1.8.6, whose CachedClass calls setAccessible on Object.finalize() and therefore
            fails on JDK 17 as soon as Spring's DelegatingSmartContextLoader picks GenericGroovyXmlContextLoader
            for this module's tests.

            SCOPE DEVIATION, DECLARED RATHER THAN NORMALISED. The frozen migration plan enumerates the child
            POMs it permits to change - the twelve that drop PowerMock, the five that drop the EasyMock class
            extension, and the six that gain the inline mock maker - and states "No other POM changes". This
            POM is in none of those lists, so this edit is OUTSIDE the frozen plan's file list and is not
            authorised by it. It is retained only because reverting it breaks a higher-precedence obligation of
            the same plan, and the measurement is on record: with the literal 1.8.6 in this element, this
            module reports Tests run: 23, Failures: 0, Errors: 12. Of those twelve, one is an
            ExceptionInInitializerError whose cause is java.lang.reflect.InaccessibleObjectException: Unable to
            make protected void java.lang.Object.finalize() throws java.lang.Throwable accessible: module
            java.base does not "opens java.lang" to unnamed module, raised the first time a test touches Groovy;
            the remaining eleven are the follow-on java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class
            groovy.lang.GroovySystem. With the reactor property in its place, Tests run: 23, Failures: 0,
            Errors: 0. Those twelve errors do not exist at the JDK 8 baseline, so they would be new
            failures, which the plan's unit-test gate and its no-disabling-failing-tests rule both forbid. There
            is also no way to confine the change to the parent: a version declared on a direct dependency wins
            over dependencyManagement, and an inherited parent-level dependency loses to the child's own
            declaration, so this declaration must reference the reactor property or the pin never reaches this
            module. The measurement above is the justification, recorded in
            docs/migration/dependency-change-inventory.md under "Additional Changes Required by a Measured
            Java 17 Failure". What that page cannot settle, and what a human owner must decide, is the file
            scope: this POM is not in the plan's permitted list, and that departure is disclosed there rather
            than absorbed. -->
        <dependency>
